From 31e06d86cb7a9e55562def6d72a09fd81254fbbd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 02:52:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/24] Match the built-in display's white to the room light
 (True Tone)

macOS moves the display's white point with the colour temperature of the
room on Macs whose ambient light sensor reports colour. On Linux the
MacBook Pro 14" M4's sensor (aop-als) already exposes the illuminance and
a colour temperature channel, and Hyprland's CTM protocol, through the
hyprsunset Omarchy runs for night light, can move the white.

omarchy-mac-truetone reads both channels and moves hyprsunset's
temperature toward a white between the panel's own and the room's, in
steps, from a per-model table under /usr/share/omarchy-mac/truetone. It
writes only above the point Omarchy reads as night light (6000 K) and
never while night light holds a lower temperature, so the two never
fight and the night light indicator stays right. A Mac with no table, or
no colour channel, is left alone. "omarchy-mac-truetone off" gives the
panel its own white back; "on" resumes. A user unit runs it with the
graphical session.
